  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a plasmonic nanoparticle can both absorb and emit light through electronic and vibronic processes. In the left schematic, an incoming photon excites a metallic nanosphere surrounded by a dashed electromagnetic near field. Electrons near the particle surface are indicated, and the particle appears to support an internal oscillation or redistribution of charge. This excitation is associated with a molecule or adsorbate near the top of the particle, suggesting interaction between the nanoparticle’s surface plasmon and molecular states. The particle can then re-radiate light, shown as transmitted or emitted green light on the right.\n\nThe middle energy diagram shows the key electronic picture: occupied metal states below the Fermi level and higher-energy surface-plasmon-related states above it. Optical excitation promotes an electron upward into these plasmonic states.\n\nThe right panel adds a configurational view with two potential-energy surfaces, labeled \\(E_0\\) and \\(E_1\\), along nuclear coordinates. Multiple vibrational levels and vertical arrows indicate Franck–Condon-type optical transitions, implying that the observed optical response involves both electronic excitation and vibrational relaxation. Overall, the figure links plasmon excitation in a nanoparticle to coupled electron, molecular, and vibrational dynamics.",
